winex11, win32u, server: Pen tablets under XWayland: wintab packets and Windows Ink pen pointers.

Under XWayland the XInput1 device events wintab selected almost never arrive (WineHQ bug 53052):
a whole stroke gave one packet, no pressure. Pens now come from XInput2 raw events of the tablet
devices (floating slave pointers with an "Abs Pressure" axis), selected on the root window once
per process and again when devices are added; raw events don't take the core events away from
the windows, so the pen keeps moving the mouse.

wintab: each axis is passed as 0..1 of its XInput2 range and mapped to the XInput1 range wintab
reports (XWayland's XInput2 x/y range is 16 times larger). The packets go to the context owner
under the cursor (raw events have no window), and a WT_PROXIMITY is sent when the pen's events
start again, since XInput1 proximity events don't come either. The pressure axis has no unit
(TU_NONE, resolution 0, like the Wacom driver): Illustrator turned TU_INCHES with a resolution of 1
into zero pressure.

Windows Ink: the process owning the window under the cursor sends WM_POINTERDOWN/UPDATE/UP
hardware input with WINE_POINTER_MESSAGE_FLAG_PEN and the pressure and tilt in the high 32 bits of
lparam; wineserver queues them with an IMDT_PEN source, without the mouse messages it synthesizes
for touch (the pen moves the mouse itself) and without repeating them. win32u keeps the pen data
of the pointer, strips it from wparam, and returns it from GetPointerPenInfo and friends.

Tested with tools/pen.py (a uinput stylus): Photoshop and Illustrator (wintab) and After Effects
(Windows Ink) draw strokes whose width follows the pressure; InDesign and Premiere Pro get the
pen as a pen pointer. wine/tests/pen/inkprobe.c logs what an Ink application reads.

